---
/**
 * One chart a corpus node draws, at the end of the prose field its `series:` entry names.
 *
 * The chart is one of `crates/series.json`'s computations rendered server-side — see
 * `renderToString` — and the node's own `figures:` block holds what a reader can quote off it to
 * the prose it sits under (`corpusSeries.ts`). Nothing here reads the corpus: the route resolves
 * the binding and hands over what to draw, so this is a placement and not a lookup.
 *
 * `level` is the heading depth the card puts the chart at. A findings card opens with its own
 * `<h2>`, so its chart is an `<h3>`; a description card has no heading of its own and its prose
 * opens at `<h2>` — see `levelHeadings` in `semantics.ts` — so a chart there is an `<h2>` too, or
 * the page would skip a level and the dist scan for that would fail the build. The heading
 * carries no `id`, deliberately: that is how `levelHeadings` tells a template heading from a
 * prose one, and the address is on the card.
 *
 * A **cloud** arrives as more than one panel. Its panel labels are captions rather than headings:
 * a panel is not a section of the document, it is one of a pair a reader is looking at together,
 * and giving each a heading would put two entries in the outline for one figure — as well as
 * pushing the next real heading a level down for no structural reason.
 *
 * A **spread** arrives with three things a column and a cloud do not have, and each is a rule
 * rather than a decoration:
 *
 * - a **legend**, because its marks are coloured by a classification. Hue alone is never identity
 *   on this site, and a hue whose meaning is only in the prose is a hue a reader has to hold in
 *   their head across five panels.
 * - a **census**, because a spread has no extreme and no fitted line: how many are in a region is
 *   the only number a reader can take off one, and every row of it is a figure the node binds and
 *   the figure gate checks three ways. It is a table rather than a caption because it is the whole
 *   of what the picture asserts.
 * - the **unreached**, because a count is a count of a population. A subject the computation could
 *   not place is named here rather than dropped out of the denominator — a spread of 607 captioned
 *   609 is a small lie that is easy to ship.
 *
 * A **band chart** arrives with a legend and an unreached list and no census, which is why
 * `unreachedSays` exists. The spread's phrase presumes one — "not in any count above" is a
 * sentence about the table — and a band chart's absences are not subjects it failed to place but
 * *floors its source has no column for*. Same rule, different sentence, and the caller supplies
 * the sentence rather than this template guessing from the shape of the props.
 */
interface Props {
  chart: {
    key: string;
    label: string;
    note: string;
    panels: { label: string | null; html: string }[];
    /** What each hue means, and the lines drawn under the marks. Present on a classed chart. */
    legend?: { swatch: string; label: string }[];
    /** One region of the picture and how many subjects are in it. */
    census?: { label: string; subjects: string }[];
    /** Named, because the alternative is a denominator nobody can reproduce. */
    unreached?: { label: string; why: string }[];
    /** What the unreached list is, in a phrase. Defaults to the census's sentence. */
    unreachedSays?: string;
  };
  level: "h2" | "h3";
}
const { chart, level } = Astro.props;
const Heading = level;
---

<div class="series" data-series={chart.key}>
  <Heading>{chart.label}</Heading>
  {
    chart.legend && chart.legend.length > 0 && (
      <div class="legend">
        {chart.legend.map((key) => (
          <span>
            <i class={`sw ${key.swatch}`} />
            {key.label}
          </span>
        ))}
      </div>
    )
  }
  <div class="panels">
    {
      chart.panels.map((panel) => (
        <div class="panel">
          {panel.label && <p class="panel-label">{panel.label}</p>}
          <div class="chartwrap" data-chart={chart.key} set:html={panel.html} />
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </div>
  <p class="note">{chart.note}</p>
  {
    chart.census && chart.census.length > 0 && (
      <div class="scroll">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Region of the picture</th>
              <th scope="col" class="tnum">
                Districts
              </th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {chart.census.map((region) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">{region.label}</th>
                <td class="tnum">{region.subjects}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    )
  }
  {
    chart.unreached && chart.unreached.length > 0 && (
      <p class="note">
        {chart.unreachedSays ?? "Not drawn, and not in any count above"}:{" "}
        {chart.unreached.map((missing, at) => (
          <>
            {at > 0 && "; "}
            <strong>{missing.label}</strong> — {missing.why}
          </>
        ))}
        .
      </p>
    )
  }
</div>
